      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "TextEncodeH3Edit"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "Treat <Picture 1> through <Picture 7> as complementary semantic survey views with equal semantic evidence of one physical institutional art studio, never as seven designs, source frames, or compositions to copy. No picture is a master reference and no picture receives priority because of its ordinal. Reconcile repeated evidence across every view; use the clearest local observation only for the detail it reveals. Together the pictures establish the room proportions, long frosted-window wall, double doors and transoms, side windows, curtain-side corner, ceiling height, fluorescent fixtures, pale walls, light-brown wooden floor, gray wall board, open floor area, central dark wooden easel, easel storage, furniture, cream shag rug, floor relationships, cream cube ottoman, and construction details. Reconstruct one recognizable room in one coherent world coordinate system. Regenerate it as high-fidelity photorealistic architectural photography with crisp natural detail, accurate perspective, physically plausible materials, neutral color, soft daylight through frosted glass, controlled highlights, realistic global illumination, clean shadow detail, and subtle believable wear. Do not reproduce source blur, softness, compression, noise, clipped windows, color casts, exposure inconsistency, lens distortion, or accidental cropping. Target object: the exact cream/off-white leather or vinyl cube ottoman centered on the cream shag rug, with slightly rounded padded edges and its horizontal upholstery seam. Preserve its dimensions, seam placement, upholstery texture, edge shape, rug relationship, and fixed floor position. Lock the cube rigidly to one world-space orientation: the same faces must remain aimed toward the same doors, windows, walls, floorboards, and rug fibers in every shot. The cube never spins, yaws, turns, or pivots; only the camera changes position. Keep it visible in every room-establishing shot. In every contextual cube study, frame the complete cube at roughly 25 to 45 percent of the image while retaining its rug or floor contact and at least two recognizable room anchors such as a door, window, wall, easel, or furniture grouping. Use multiple three-quarter, profile, low, high, top-surface, seam, material, contact-point, and environmental viewpoints, with changing background parallax proving that the camera moved around the stationary cube. Never create an isolated product shot, macro-only crop, blank background, or framing that removes the room. Freeze the regenerated room and every object after establishment; only camera placement, height, focal length, and framing may change across instantaneous hard cuts.",
        "generate | semantic Picture 1 (FL2VA)",
        "none (source only)",
        1344,
        768,
        "crop center",
        "directed | room and object study cuts",
        1024,
        "match output area",
        "scene coverage | 362-frame camera path",
        16,
        360,
        "clockwise / camera right",
        5,
        false
      ]
    },
